      <ArticleTitle>Macrophage-derived TGF-<i>&#x3b2;</i>1 drives portal fibrosis in murine NASH.</ArticleTitle>
      <Abstract>
        <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND AND AIMS" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Portal fibrosis predicts outcome in nonalcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH), but the cellular source of profibrotic signals is unresolved.</AbstractText>
        <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We used myeloid-specific Tgfb1 knockout mice fed a choline-deficient high-fat diet (CDAHFD) for 12 weeks (n = 10 per group) and validated findings in 48 human liver biopsies.</AbstractText>
        <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Myeloid Tgfb1 deletion reduced Sirius red-positive area by 38% (p = 0.002) and lowered portal collagen I deposition. Human portal macrophage density correlated with fibrosis stage (r = 0.61, p &lt; 0.001).</AbstractText>
        <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Macrophage-derived TGF-beta1 is a targetable driver of portal fibrosis in NASH.</AbstractText>
      </Abstract>
